    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY. (1) It does not say QF_NIA is certified. It covers single-variable polynomial EQUALITIES only. Multivariate NIA, inequalities of degree >= 3, and |a0| past the checker's scan bound (2^20) stay DECIDED BUT NOT CERTIFIED -- and that bound is deliberate: the checker proves divisor-set completeness by scanning 1..=|a0| precisely so it does not reuse the producer's sqrt-with-cofactors enumeration, which is where a completeness bug would hide. Reusing it would raise coverage and lower what coverage means. (2) It does not say an external checker has read the artifact. The re-derivation is in-tree, so this is the self-checker tier, not the external-artifact tier; Lean reconstruction of these certificates is not done. (3) It does not say the certificate is minimal or canonical. (4) The negative-discriminant case is NOT covered here -- it keeps its own certificate, because an autogenesis operation is registered against that shape and redefining a pinned shape is not an upgrade. HOW HARD THE CHECKER WAS TESTED. Nine guards, each deleted in turn; all nine now kill exactly one test. The first run killed only five: four guards were removable with everything green, because another guard rejected the same forgery first. Isolating them needed certificates the producer would never emit -- notably a degree-2 argument over `x^3+x^2+x-3 = 0`, which is SATISFIABLE at x=1 and whose leading three coefficients give a genuine non-square 13 with a valid bracket, so every other guard passes and only the degree check prevents certifying a satisfiable query as unsat.",
